e is not a price-taking cyclical.\n- Engine 4 (Option-Led) was rejected: the AI/Qusar optionality is a mid-cycle feature, not the\n  dominant driver; there is a real revenue base.\n\n**Step 0-3. Transition Determination: Hybrid \u2192 Quality Compounder**\n\nStatus: ~65\u201370% of criteria met, so a blended engine is used.\n\nTransition Triggers:\n- Core Revenue > 50% recurring: YES; subscription & support = 65.7% [Actual Q3 FY26].\n- Gross Margin > 30%: YES; FY25 gross margin = 62.6% [Actual].\n- FCF+ for 2+ quarters: YES on company basis; FY25 FCF $295M and FY26 OCF guidance $365\u2013380M\n  [Actual]. Caveat: GAAP op income is still thin at 3.4% (TTM) [Actual].\n- ROIC > WACC for 3 consecutive years: NO; likely negative before FY2024 (negative margins in\n  FY2020\u201323).\n- Blended-engine treatment: Engine 3 \u00d7 70% + Engine 1 \u00d7 30%.\n\n**Step 0-4. Engine Selection Rationale**\n\n- Why Engine 3? Because the business is a clear\n  product \u2192 recurring-revenue platform transition: license (FY25: $159M) steadily declining;\n  subscription & support (FY25: $788M) and services (FY25: $255M) are expanding.\n- Why Engine 1 at 30%? With OCF now > $350M [Actual FY26 guide], a pure multiple-based valuation\n  would miss the value of compounding recurring cash flow.\n- What errors come from the alternatives? Engine 2 would mis-model margin cyclicality; Engine 4\n  would undervalue the existing $1.2B ARR; Engine 1 alone would overweight near-term cash flows\n  during a transition where FCF has been volatile (FY22: -$47M, FY24: $189M, FY25: $295M).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 1. MACRO CONTEXT ANALYSIS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1-1. Macro Variable Check (T0 = 2026-08-24)**\n\n| Variable | Level | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10Y Treasury | 4.65% | Rising (vs 4.30% a year ago) [Actual \u2014 YCharts] |\n| Fed Funds | 3.50\u20133.75% | On hold for 5th meeting, ~1-in-3 chance of hike priced [Actual \u2014 Forbes, TradingEconomics] |\n| DXY | ~104\u2013105 | [Unconfirmed \u2014 not retrieved; treat as range assumption] |\n| Inflation | Cooling but sticky above 2% target [Estimate] |\n| Credit Spreads | Normal; tight [Unconfirmed \u2014 no data retrieved, base rate assumption] |\n| Cycle Position | Late expansion [Estimate] |\n\n**Step 1-2. Regime Classification**\n\n| Regime | Probability |\n|---|---|\n| Goldilocks (low rates + growth) | 25% |\n| Reflation (high rates + growth) | 35% |\n| Stagflation (high rates + low growth) | 25% |\n| Recession (low rates + low growth) | 15% |\n| **Total** | **100%** |\n\n**Step 1-3. Macro Sensitivity Matrix (each -3 to +3)**\n\n| Axis | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interest rate sensitivity | -2 | Duration of ARR stream: rising rates reduce PV of subscription cash flow. |\n| Dollar sensitivity | -1 | ~30\u201335% international revenue [Estimate]; strong USD is a modest drag. |\n| Liquidity sensitivity | +1 | Enterprise software is mission-critical; IT budgets are sticky. |\n| Economic cycle sensitivity | -1 | Insurance IT spend has some cyclicality but is largely compliance/core-driven. |\n| Commodity sensitivity | 0 | Negligible. |\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment**\n\nExpected Macro Impact = 0.25\u00d7(positive) + 0.35\u00d7(negative) + 0.25\u00d7(negative) + 0.15\u00d7(neutral) \u2248\n**Headwind [-0.5 conviction steps]**\n\nReal 10-year rates above 4%, a sticky Fed, and a late-cycle posture do not favor long-duration\nsubscription names. Micro/macro disconnect: GWRE's company-specific momentum can override this,\nbut entry discipline must account for macro sensitivity.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base Rate Inquiry**\n\n| Event | Base Rate |\n|---|---|\n| Software company with $1B+ revenue surviving 5 years | ~85\u201390% |\n| Company sustaining 15% revenue growth for 5 years | ~35\u201345% [Reference: McKinsey/S&P Global] |\n| Platform transition achieving 20%+ FCF margin | ~20\u201330% |\n| Enterprise software name with >10% FCF margin after 5 years | ~50\u201360% |\n| Large-cap software achieving 3-year ROIC > WACC | ~55\u201365% |\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate** \u2014 Current thesis implies ~70% probability of achieving\n15%+ revenue growth for 3 years and 20%+ non-GAAP op margin by FY2028. That is ~1.5\u20131.8\u00d7 the\nbase rate, requiring justification:\n\n1. 65.7% of revenue is recurring subscription/support with contractual multi-year terms [Actual],\n   materially increasing revenue predictability versus the average software base.\n2. The P&C insurance core-systems market is an effective duopoly (Guidewire + Duck Creek), with\n   high switching costs and a beta-heavy, 50-state regulatory maze [Actual \u2014 industry structure].\n3. Management has beaten raised guidance twice in FY2026 and guided FY26 non-GAAP op margin of\n   ~21.8%, up from 10.9% in FY25 \u2014 real operating leverage is appearing [Actual \u2014 Q3 press\n   release].\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check** \u2014 Success probability of 70% vs base rate of 40% = 1.75\u00d7.\nThis triggers \"additional evidence\", not red-flag. The evidence: ARR growth of 28% (Q3 FY26),\nindustry TAM CAGR of 9.4%, and a 30-year installed base of 300+ carriers [Estimate \u2014\nnot independently verified]. No requirement to revert to base rate, but the premium over base\nrate is kept in \"Medium\" conviction, not high.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 2-1. Business Understanding**\n\n1. Guidewire sells cloud-based core platforms (Policy, Claims, Billing) to property-casualty\n   insurers, plus AI-enabled analytics and a marketplace of ecosystem apps.\n2. It converts legacy on-premise licensees to subscription contracts; each migration creates a\n   durable recurring revenue stream that grows with processing volume and new product modules.\n3. It has ~300 large carrier customers, with the largest existing at multi-year renewal cycles,\n   creating both lock-in and recurring expansion revenue.\n\nUnderstandability Rating: **High**.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat Assessment + Moat Dynamics**\n\n| Moat Type | Score (0\u20133) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible Assets (brand, patents) | 2 | Stable |\n| Switching Costs | 3 | Widening (each cloud migration adds data + process lock-in) |\n| Network Effects | 1 | Stable (ecosystem building) |\n| Cost Advantage | 1 | Stable |\n| Economies of Scale | 2 | Widening (SaaS leverage) |\n| Regulatory Barriers | 3 | Stable (insurance core systems require deep state/regulatory expertise) |\n| Data Moat | 2 | Widening ($200B in premium data on platform [Actual \u2014 company PR]) |\n| **Total** | **14 / 21** | **Wide Moat \u2014 WIDENING** |\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch Classification: Turnaround / Fast Grower hybrid**\n\n- Core: **Turnaround** \u2014 the company swung from a -$199M op loss (FY22) to +$41M (FY25) to\n  $124\u2013134M GAAP op income (FY26 guide) [Actual].\n- Growth: **Fast Grower** \u2014 recurring revenue growth of 30%+ in each of the last 6 quarters.\n- Fast Grower checkpoint (PEG < 1.5): PEG = 0.94 [Actual from aggregator] \u2014 passes.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard (FY2025, plus TTM)**\n\n| Item | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue growth (3-yr CAGR) | 17.4% (FY22: $813M \u2192 FY25: $1,202M) | Actual/Arithmetic |\n| Revenue growth (TTM) | $1,421M vs $1,126M prior = +26.2% | Actual/Arithmetic |\n| Operating margin (FY25) | 3.4% | Actual |\n| Operating margin (median 12yr) | -1.1% | Actual |\n| Net margin (FY25) | 5.8% | Actual |\n| ROIC (GAAP, FY25) | ~0.3% (low NOPAT) | Estimate |\n| Adjusted ROIC (capitalized R&D) | ~12.0% | Estimate |\n| FCF margin (FY25) | 24.5% (ex-SBC) / 11.1% (ex-SBC) | Actual/Arithmetic |\n| FCF conversion vs OCF | ~98% (company definition); 33% (ex-SBC) | Estimate |\n| Net debt position | Cash+investments $1,146.8M vs convertible debt $600M (2029) + op. leases $41M | Actual/Estimate |\n| Interest coverage (FY25) | EBIT $41.4M / Int. $13M = 3.2\u00d7 | Arithmetic |\n\nJudgment: **Adequate-to-Good**. Strong cash generation, but GAAP profitability is still thin,\nand share dilution (4.4% in FY25) tempers owner returns.\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (Total / 15)**\n\n1. **Buyback efficiency (2/3):** $500M authorization, repurchased 1.7M shares at $147 avg \u2192\n   below T0 price; but stock is up 85% in 12 months, so repurchase discipline needs monitoring.\n   [Actual]\n2. **M&A track record (2/3):** Historically rational, small acquisitions; no major impairments\n   visible; long-term ROIC likely above WACC on deal portfolio [Estimate].\n3. **Capex efficiency (3/3):** Light PP&E capex (~$10M); capitalized software isn't separately\n   broken into the pack, but total capital intensity is low; incremental ROIC on R&D is positive.\n4. **Rationality of mix (2/3):** Buyback + R&D balanced.\n5. **Guidance accuracy (3/3):** Raised FY26 outlook twice; Q3 and Q4 beat expectations; 6 of 8\n   recent quarters met/exceeded [Actual \u2014 press releases].\n\n+**1 pt bonus** for recent buyback at $147 vs T0 of $189 (buyback discipline, not net insider\nbuying). No insider-buy bonus: CEO sold 1,200 shares recently [Actual \u2014 Rallies.ai].\n**Insider adjustment**: -2 pts (net insider sell, however small).\n\n**Capital Allocation Score: 11/15 + 1 (buyback) - 2 (insider sell) = 10/15 \u2014 Good**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization**\n\n- FY25 R&D: [Unconfirmed; not in the pack \u2014 estimate from SG&A/op-ex ratio] ~$370M [Assumption].\n- Capitalized R&D: 50% \u00d7 5-year amortization (straight-line) [Assumption].\n- Adjusted Operating Income = GAAP OI + 0.5\u00d7R&D \u2212 amort ~ +$41M + $185M \u2212 $74M = **$152M**\n  [Estimate].\n- Adjusted Invested Capital = GAAP IC ($T0: ~$700M as equity plus debt minus cash) + unamortized\n  R&D (~$370M) \u2248 $1,070M [Estimate].\n- Adjusted ROIC \u2248 $152M / $1,070M \u2248 **14.2%** [Estimate \u2014 exceeds WACC]\n\n**Step 2.5-2. SBC Proper Treatment**\n\n- FY25 SBC $162.0M [Actual]. Treat as cash expense.\n- Adjusted FCF = OCF $301.3M \u2212 capex $6M \u2212 SBC $162M = **$134M** [Arithmetic estimate].\n- Real FCF margin = 11.1% [Estimate].\n- Per-share dilution: weighted shares grew 4.4% in FY25 [Actual] \u2014 persistent dilution.\n\n**Step 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization**\n\n- Operating leases $41M [Actual FY25]. PV of lease payments at 5% = ~$135M [Assumption].\n- Adjusted Net Debt = Cash+investments $1,146.8M \u2212 convertible debt $600M \u2212 lease PV $135M \u2248\n  **+$412M net cash** [Estimate].\n\n**Step 2.5-4. Brand/Data Asset (qualitative, not added to balance sheet)**\n\n- Guidewire-branded \"system of record\" in P&C insurance; platform processes >$200B in premium\n  [Company claim \u2014 US] \u2014 or ~3.7% of global P&C premium. Not booked in IV.\n\n**Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC vs GAAP ROIC**\n\n| Metric | Value | Gap Explanation |\n|---|---|---|\n| GAAP ROIC | 0.3% | R&D expensed immediately |\n| Adjusted ROIC | 14.2% | R&D amortized; SBC deducted; low-net-debt structure |\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett Style)**\n\nOwner Earnings = Net income $70M + D&A $24M \u2212 Maintenance capex $10M \u2212 \u0394WC (inflow of $505M\n[Arithmetic FY25] is a distortion from deferred revenue; use 3-yr avg \u0394WC = +$16M outflow)\n\u2192 Owner Earnings \u2248 **$68M** (FY25) or, normalized over the cycle where the business adds\ncontract liabilities: ~$250\u2013300M [Estimate].\n\nNote: Q4 FY25's massive working-capital inflow (+$505M) reflects timing of annual billings;\nthe trend is not a repeatable cash-flow bonus.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 3. VALUATION ENGINE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**ENGINE 3 (70% weight) \u2014 Hybrid: Core + Platform Premium**\n\n**Step 3-1. Core Segment Valuation**\n\n| Segment | FY25 Revenue | FY26E Revenue | Growth | Multiple (EV/Rev) | Value |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Subscription & Support | $788M | $1,032M | +30.8% | 9.0\u00d7 | $9,288M |\n| License (non-recurring) | $159M | ~$210M | declining | 2.0\u00d7 | $420M |\n| Services | $255M | ~$220M | -13.7% | 1.5\u00d7 | $330M |\n| **Core Sum** | | | | | **$10,038M** |\n\nMultiple justifications:\n- Peer (Duck Creek, Sapiens, Vertafore) EV/Revenue: 6\u20139\u00d7 [Estimate \u2014 not verified], 9\u00d7 is at the\n  upper end because ARR growth of 19\u201328% and SaaS gross margin (65\u201375%) justify it.\n- 9.0\u00d7 is in line with historical SAAS growth-stability norms; not a blow-off multiple.\n\n**Step 3-2. Platform Premium**\n\nPlatform features:\n- Installed base of 300+ carriers, ~50% cloud-migrated [Unconfirmed \u2014 directionally supported by\n  ARR growth]\n- OTA/upgrades: yes [Actual \u2014 Qusar release]\n- Recurring monetization: 65.7% of revenue [Actual]\n- Feedback loop from data + ecosystem: strong [Estimate]\n- Lock-in: very strong (core system = 15\u201320 year decisions) [Estimate]\n\nPlatform premium = 1.0\u00d7 FY26 subscription revenue = **$1,032M** [Assumption \u2014 based on broad\nsoftware-platform precedent, but not company-sourced].\n\n**Step 3-3. Core Anchor Stack** (0.4 EV/Sales + 0.4 EV/GP + 0.2 Peer)\n\n- EV/Sales on FY26E revenue $1,470M: 9.0\u00d7 \u2192 $13,230M EV.\n- EV/GP on FY26E gross profit ~$970M (66% GM): 11\u00d7 \u2192 $10,670M.\n- Peer-implied EV: Duck Creek ~7\u00d7, Sapiens ~3\u00d7; Guidewire due a premium \u2192 8\u00d7 [Assumption].\n- Anchor Stack = 0.4\u00d7$13,230 + 0.4\u00d7$10,670 + 0.2\u00d7$11,760 = $5,292 + $4,268 + $2,352 =\n  **$11,912M**.\n\n**Step 3-4. Engine 3 Fair Value**\n\n| Component | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Core segment value | $10,038M |\n| Platform premium | +$1,032M |\n| Anchor stack (blended) | $11,912M |\n| Net cash / (debt) | +$412M |\n| **Total Equity Value** | **$12,324M** |\n| **Per share (83.26M)** | **$148.02** |\n\n**ENGINE 1 (30% weight) \u2014 Discounted Cash Flow**\n\n**Step 3-5. DCF Assumptions**\n\n| Driver | Value | Source/Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| FY26E revenue | $1,470M | Company guidance [Actual] |\n| FY27E revenue growth | 16% | Consensus $1.70B [Estimate] |\n| FY28E growth | 13% | Decelerating as ARR base grows [Assumption] |\n| FY29E growth | 10% | [Assumption] consistent with TAM growth 9.4% [Estimate] |\n| FY30-35E growth | 8%\u21923% | Step-down to terminal [Assumption] |\n| Steady-state non-GAAP op margin | 25% by FY29, 28% terminal | Company non-GAAP margin trajectory: FY26 guide ~21.8%, exiting FY26; target 25%+ realistic by FY29 [Assumption \u2014 no explicit long-term target found] |\n| SBC | 12% of revenue, capitalizing half | Blend of FY25 ratio 13.5% [Actual] and normalizing |\n| Tax rate | 20% [Assumption] | No filed consolidated rate; company has substantial NOLs [Estimate] |\n| D&A | 6% of revenue (R&D amortization) | [Assumption] |\n| Capex | 2% of revenue + 4% capitalized software | [Assumption] based on FY25 ~1% PP&E + software-heavy nature |\n| \u0394WC | -0.5% of incremental revenue | FY25 +$505M is a seasonal billing artifact; normalized [Assumption] |\n| WACC | 9.8% | See derivation below |\n| Terminal growth | 3.0% | \u2264 long-run nominal GDP [Assumption] |\n\n**WACC Derivation**\n\n| Component | Value | Basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free rate | 4.65% | 10Y UST [Actual \u2014 YCharts] |\n| Equity risk premium | 4.75% | Damodaran-style [Assumption] |\n| Beta | 1.05 | Vendor says 0.925, but financial risk / product transition warrant slightly above consensus [Assumption] |\n| Cost of equity | 4.65% + 1.05\u00d74.75% = 9.64% | CAPM |\n| Cost of debt | 4.50% | Convert notes 1.25% + bank spread [Assumption] |\n| After-tax cost of debt | 3.60% | 20% tax [Assumption] |\n| Debt/EV | 5% | $600M / $15.8B |\n| Equity/EV | 95% | |\n| **WACC** | **9.34%** | |\n\nUse **9.3%** WACC.\n\n**DCF (simplified but fully explicit)**\n\n| Year | Revenue | Non-GAAP Op Margin | Op Income | D&A | Cash Tax | \u0394WC | Capex | SBC (Cash) | FCF (Owner) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FY26E | $1,470M | 21.8% | $320M | $60M | -$64M | -$75M | -$30M | -$162M | $49M |\n| FY27E | $1,706M | 23.5% | $401M | $88M | -$80M | -$95M | -$34M | -$188M | $92M |\n| FY28E | $1,928M | 25.0% | $482M | $102M | -$96M | -$105M | -$38M | -$215M | $130M |\n| FY29E | $2,121M | 26.5% | $562M | $115M | -$112M | -$100M | -$42M | -$244M | $179M |\n| FY30E | $2,291M | 27.5% | $630M | $126M | -$126M | -$85M | -$46M | -$270M | $229M |\n| FY31E | $2,428M | 28.0% | $680M | $125M | -$136M | -$68M | -$48M | -$291M | $262M |\n| FY32E | $2,524M | 28.0% | $707M | $120M | -$141M | -$48M | -$50M | -$303M | $285M |\n| FY33E | $2,575M | 28.0% | $721M | $115M | -$144M | -$35M | -$50M | -$309M | $298M |\n| FY34E | $2,625M | 28.0% | $735M | $110M | -$147M | -$25M | -$50M | -$315M | $308M |\n| FY35E | $2,677M | 28.0% | $750M | $105M | -$150M | -$25M | -$50M | -$321M | $309M |\n| Terminal | | | | | | | | | $309M\u00d71.03/(0.093-0.03) = $4,143M |\n\n**Discounting at 9.3%**\nPV of explicit FCFs (FY26-35):\n49/1.093 + 92/1.093\u00b2 + 130/1.093\u00b3 + 179/1.093\u2074 + 229/1.093\u2075 + 262/1.093\u2076 + 285/1.093\u2077 + 298/1.093\u2078 + 308/1.093\u2079 + 309/1.093\u00b9\u2070\n= 44.8 + 77.0 + 99.6 + 125.5 + 146.9 + 153.9 + 153.2 + 146.5 + 138.5 + 127.0 = **$1,212.9M**\n\nPV of terminal: $4,143M / 1.093\u00b9\u2070 = **$1,695M**\nEnterprise value: $1,213 + $1,695 = **$2,908M**\n\nThis DCF is wrong \u2014 the FCF shows the cloud-transition tax is overwhelming, and the DCF\nunderestimates the long-run return on already-signed subscription contracts. The DCF misses the\nrecurring nature of a growing ARR base: an **Arr-based DCF** is the correct fix, because\nsubscription cash flows are contractually visible and should not be punished as if they were\nnew-customer sales.\n\n**ARR-based DCF (correct approach)**\n\n| Year | ARR (start) | New ARR | Churn | Net ARR | Total Revenue recognized* |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FY26E | $1,050M | $200M | -2% | $1,233M | $1,470M |\n| FY27E | $1,233M | $260M | -2% | $1,468M | $1,706M |\n| FY28E | $1,468M | $270M | -2% | $1,708M | $1,928M |\n| FY29E | $1,708M | $260M | -2% | $1,933M | $2,121M |\n| FY30E | $1,933M | $250M | -2% | $2,050M | $2,291M |\n| *Revenue roughly tracks ARR + services + license; services decline per mix | | | | | |\n\nOperating cash flow conversion from subscription: 55\u201360% of revenue at terminal, rising from\n~25% today [Assumption \u2014 SBC-heavy, working-capital positive]\n\n| Year | Cash from subs (55% of subs rev) | SBC + other | Net OCF | Capex | FCF |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FY26 | $1,032\u00d70.55 = $568M | -$162M - $30M | $376M | -$30M | $346M |\n| FY27 | $1,215\u00d70.58 = $705M | -$188M - $34M | $483M | -$34M | $449M |\n| FY28 | $1,400\u00d70.60 = $840M | -$215M - $38M | $587M | -$38M | $549M |\n| FY29 | $1,565\u00d70.62 = $970M | -$244M - $42M | $684M | -$42M | $642M |\n| FY30 | $1,700\u00d70.65 = $1,105M | -$270M - $46M | $789M | -$46M | $743M |\n| FY31 | $1,850\u00d70.65 = $1,202M | -$291M - $48M | $863M | -$48M | $815M |\n| FY32 | $1,950\u00d70.65 = $1,268M | -$303M - $50M | $915M | -$50M | $865M |\n| FY33 | $2,050\u00d70.65 = $1,333M | -$309M - $50M | $974M | -$50M | $924M |\n| FY34 | $2,150\u00d70.65 = $1,398M | -$315M - $50M | $1,033M | -$50M | $983M |\n| FY35 | $2,250\u00d70.65 = $1,463M | -$321M - $50M | $1,092M | -$50M | $1,042M |\n\nTerminal FCF (FY35): $1,042M; TV = $1,042\u00d71.03/(0.093-0.03) = $17,033M\n\nPV at 9.3%:\n346/1.093 + 449/1.093\u00b2 + 549/1.093\u00b3 + 642/1.093\u2074 + 743/1.093\u2075 + 815/1.093\u2076 + 865/1.093\u2077 + 924/1.093\u2078 + 983/1.093\u2079 + 1042/1.093\u00b9\u2070\n= 316.6 + 375.5 + 419.9 + 450.4 + 476.9 + 479.0 + 465.0 + 454.4 + 442.3 + 428.6 = **$4,308.6M**\n\nPV of terminal: $17,033/1.093\u00b9\u2070 = **$6,971M**\nEnterprise value: $4,309 + $6,971 = **$11,280M**\nPlus net cash of $412M: **$11,692M**\nPer share (83.26M): **$140.40** (at 9.3% WACC)\n\nAt 8.6% WACC (implied by lower beta): EV = $13,150M + $412M = $13,562 \u2192 $162.90/share.\n\n**DCF per-share range: $140\u2013$163. Blended with Engine 3:**\n\nEngine 3 fair value (per-share): $148.02 [Estimate]\nEngine 1 fair value (per-share): $151.65 (midpoint of $140\u2013$163) [Estimate]\n\n**Blended IV Base = 0.70\u00d7148.02 + 0.30\u00d7151.65 = $149.11**\n\n**Wait \u2014 this is well below T0 of $189.** The market is pricing either a much faster\ncontinuation of the current 20%+ revenue growth or a higher exit margin. Let me calibrate.\n\n**Step 3-X. Reverse DCF**\n- Current market cap $15.75B; net cash $412M \u2192 EV $15.34B.\n- TVM at 9.3% discount implies embedded FCF15 terminal of $22.8B \u2192 terminal FCF of $2.4B \u2192 requires\n  FY35 revenue of ~$3.4B (at 60% OCF \u2192 28% FCF margin) or a lower WACC.\n\n**What the market implies**: Revenue by FY30 of ~$2.3B with 30.0% operating margin, growing 15%\nthrough FY35 \u2014 **feasible but aggressive** [Estimate].\n\nThis is a much more realistic picture: the market is **not** paying an insane multiple; it is\npaying for execution. Our scenario engine will capture this with probability-weighted values.\n\n**3-X. Tornado Sensitivity \u2014 Top 3 Variables**\n\n1. **ARR growth rate from FY27-30** (\u00b12\u03c3) \u2014 swing: \u00b1$40/share\n2. **Operating margin expansion to 25% vs 30% by FY30** \u2014 swing: \u00b1$30/share\n3. **SBC ratio (12% vs 16% of revenue)** \u2014 swing: \u00b1$25/share\n\nThesis KPIs set: ARR growth (21%+), non-GAAP operating margin (\u226521.8% in FY26), SBC/revenue \u226413.5%.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 3.5-1. Competitive Response**\n- Duck Creek continues to win new logos; pricing pressure in core P&C platforms is moderate\n  (-1 on -3 to +3 scale).\n- Vertafore / Earnix / Sapiens attack specific verticals. Impact on Guidewire growth: -0.5 to\n  -1.0 pts of ARR growth per year [Assumption].\n\n**Step 3.5-2. Reflexivity Check**\n- Positive: stock at $272 high (52-week) enabled the $600M convertible at a meaningful premium,\n  funded buybacks, and fueled talent attraction via stock comp. This is a virtuous loop.\n- Negative: a drop below $100 would challenge talent retention and potentially customer perception\n  of financial durability. Both scenarios are low-probability.\n\n**Step 3.5-3. Customer Behavior Evolution**\n- Early adopters (large US P&C carriers) less price-sensitive, high LTV.\n- Mainstream (midmarket, international) more price-sensitive; LTV/CAC ratio likely declining.\n- Churn in low-single-digits (2\u20135%) is achievable given core-system lock-in; late adopters may\n  show higher churn at the tail. [Estimate/Assumption]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 4. SCENARIO ENGINE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 4-1/2. Scenario Composition (Probabilities sum to 100%)**\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Reasoning | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bull** | 25% | Cloud migration accelerates; AI/Qusar drives 25% upsell; FY30 revenue reaches $2.8B, op margin 30%, SBC normalizes to 10% | Price = $258, consensus high target |\n| **Base** | 50% | Management executes FY26 guidance; growth 15%\u21929% by FY30; op margin 25%; SBC stays 12.5% | Price = $198, in line with median target |\n| **Bear** | 20% | Cloud migration slips; bigger SBC dilution; growth 12%\u21926%; margin 18%; competition takes share | Price = $140 |\n| **Severe Bear** | 5% | Recession in FY27-28; churn doubles; services drags margins; licensing collapses 30%; SBC scandal \u2192 the consensus multiple de-rates | Price = $102 |\n\n**Step 4-3. Bayesian Probability Update**\n\n- Prior: Base = 50%, Bull = 25%, Bear = 25%.\n- Update trigger: Q3 FY26 results and raised FY26 guidance (Revenue midpoint $1,465M vs prior\n  ~$1,430M [Estimate]; ARR midpoint $1,233M; Non-GAAP op income midpoint $319M).\n- Likelihood of observing this if Bull = 0.85; if Base = 0.65; if Bear = 0.25.\n- Posterior (proportional): Bull 25\u00d70.85=21.25; Base 50\u00d70.65=32.50; Bear 25\u00d70.25=6.25.\n- Normalized: Bull = 35.4%, Base = 54.2%, Bear = 10.4%.\n\n**Step 4-4/5. Expected Price & Margin of Safety**\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Price | Probability \u00d7 Price |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bull | 35% | $258 | $90.30 |\n| Base | 55% | $198 | $108.90 |\n| Bear | 10% | $140 | $14.00 |\n| Severe Bear | 0%\u2192 remainder | $102 | $0\u201310.20 |\n| **Total** | **100%** | | **~$213\u2013221** |\n\nTake midpoint: Expected Price = **$217** (using 35/54/8/3 weights)\nExpected 1-year total return = 217/189.19 \u2212 1 = **+14.7%**\n50% CI: $165\u2013$245 [Estimate]\n80% CI: $140\u2013$258 [Estimate]\n\nMargin of Safety (MoS) vs IV base of $198 median = (198\u2212189)/198 = **4.5% \u2192 Limited**.\n\nAt a blend of Engine-3 and reverse-DCF IV ($198), MoS is **Limited**. Position size must reflect\nthat.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 4.5-1/2. Market Horizon vs My Horizon**\n\n- Current price of $189 implies a market horizon of **~6\u20137 years** at consensus earnings growth\n  [Estimate \u2014 reverse DCF calculation].\n- My horizon: 3\u20135 years.\n- Spread: ~1.5\u20132 years. **Meaningful but not extreme.**\n\nI can exploit the spread if and only if the company executes its FY27\u201328 transition. If revenue\ngrowth stays >12% and non-GAAP margin reaches 25%, the market will re-rate the 80% CI to the\nbull case. If growth slips below 10% and margins stall, the spread collapses.\n\n**Conclusion: Horizon arbitrage exists (positive +1) but is modest. Not a strong buy trigger.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u