ty | \u22121 | Net-cash balance sheet; but long-duration multiple is rate-sensitive |\n| Dollar Sensitivity | \u22121 | International revenue + FX swings (Q3 FY26 had $20.1M FX loss) |\n| Liquidity Sensitivity | \u22121 | High-multiple software compresses when liquidity tightens |\n| Economic Cycle Sensitivity | +1 | P&C insurance budgets are defensive/countercyclical |\n| Commodity Sensitivity | 0 | None |\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment: WEIGHTED HEADWIND.\nHigher-for-longer rates and hawkish Fed bias are a direct headwind for a\n70\u00d7 forward non-GAAP P/E software equity. Macro alignment: Headwind.\nConviction \u22120.5 steps; strengthen split-entry discipline.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates:\n\n| Question | Base Rate | Applied |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5-year survival: $1.4B-rev software leader | ~85\u201390% | High |\n| Successful platform transition (on-prem\u2192cloud) | ~20\u201330% | Guidewire has surpassed this; evidence: ARR $1.147B (+19%) [Actual] |\n| Cloud/core-system leader sustain 15%+ ARR growth for 5 yrs | ~40\u201350% | Applied |\n| Pre-revenue \u2192 $1B revenue in 5 yrs | ~3\u20135% | N/A \u2014 already past |\n| Large M&A ROIC > WACC | ~25\u201335% | Guidewire's M&A is modest and in-platform |\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate: My base case requires sustained growth\nabove industry (insurtech software CAGR 9.7% [Estimate, Technavio]) for 5 years.\nJustification: (1) dominant Tier-1 P&C share with 20-yr switching costs,\n(2) only 40-50% of on-prem base migrated \u2192 built-in upgrade cycle,\n(3) $1.2T P&C premium pool funding modernization. Justification adequate;\nno excessive deviation from base rate for success.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check: Success probability (~40%) is NOT >1.5x\nthe comparable base rate; no re-evaluation trigger.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences):\nGuidewire is the dominant provider of mission-critical core systems \u2014 Policy\nAdministration, Billing, and Claims \u2014 for property & casualty insurers,\nincluding many of the world's largest carriers. The company is converting its\nlarge on-premise installed base to Guidewire Cloud, converting one-time\nlicenses into recurring subscription and support revenue (ARR $1.147B at\nQ3 FY26). Its products sit at the center of insurance operations, creating\nmulti-year, multi-million-dollar switching costs and an unusually durable\nrecurring-revenue franchise.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat score:\n\n| Moat Type | Score (0-3) |\n|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (brand/trust) | 3 |\n| Switching costs | 3 |\n| Network effects (ecosystem/integrations) | 2 |\n| Cost advantage | 1 |\n| Economies of scale | 2 |\n| Regulatory barriers | 2 |\n| Data moat | 2 |\n| TOTAL | 15 \u2192 WIDE MOAT |\n\nMoat direction: STABLE-TO-WIDENING.\nWidening: cloud migrations add installed-base depth; AI features (Qusar)\nraise switching costs; 17-20 cloud deals per quarter [Estimate, web brief].\nNarrowing risk: Duck Creek's cloud-native, faster-implementation stack is\nwinning mid-market; AI-driven commoditization by hyperscalers could emerge.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification: Fast-Grower \u2192 Stalwart inflection.\nCheckpoints met: 3-yr rev CAGR 13.9%, +27% TTM growth, durable margins.\nKey checkpoint: must convert 19% ARR growth into 15%+ revenue growth and\nGAAP margin expansion. Not yet fully proven (GAAP OI margin only ~7% TTM).\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard:\n\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR (3-yr) | 13.9% (FY22\u2192FY25) | [Arithmetic] |\n| Revenue Growth (TTM) | +26.9% (Q3 FY26) | [Actual] |\n| GAAP Operating Margin (TTM) | ~7% | [Estimate] |\n| Non-GAAP OI Margin FY26E | 21.8% | [Actual] guidance |\n| ROE (TTM) | ~11.4% (NI $160M / avg equity $1.4B) | [Arithmetic] |\n| GAAP ROIC (FY25) | ~5% | [Estimate] |\n| FCF Margin (reported) | 23.7% TTM (OCF 351 \u2212 capex 13)/1,421 | [Arithmetic] |\n| Owner FCF Margin (after SBC) | ~11-12% TTM | [Estimate] |\n| Net Cash Position | ~$390M | [Estimate] |\n| Interest Coverage | ~8.5x GAAP OI vs interest | [Arithmetic] |\n| Balance Sheet Rating | Excellent \u2014 net cash, no near-term maturities | [Judgment] |\n\nJudgment: GOOD (excellent balance sheet; still GAAP-thin profitability due to\nSBC).\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0-3 each):\n\n| Criterion | Score | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 2 | $500M authorized Jan-26; bought 1.7M sh at avg $147.07 \u2014 below T0 but offset by 4.4% net issuance FY25 |\n| M&A track record | 2 | In-platform (Cyence); modest scale; no impairments |\n| Capex efficiency | 2 | Light P/P/E capex; capitalised software the real spend |\n| Rationality of mix | 3 | Investing + buyback + no dividend; disciplined |\n| Guidance accuracy | 2 | FY26 raised twice; prior FY22-23 misses |\n| Insider ownership bonus | 0 | Insider owns 0.35%; no 6-mo cluster buys |\n| TOTAL | 11/15 | Good |\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization: R&D is NOT split out in the pack\n[Unconfirmed \u2014 segment/expense-line detail unavailable]. Cannot capitalise\nR&D precisely; conservative treatment below.\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC treatment (the critical adjustment):\nFY25 SBC $162M [Actual] = 13.5% of revenue. This is a real recurring\nexpense. Owner FCF (true distributable cash):\n    FY25: OCF $301M \u2212 capex $6M \u2212 SBC $162M = $133M   [Arithmetic]\n    TTM (Apr-26): OCF $351M \u2212 capex $13M \u2212 SBC ~$163M \u2248 $175M   [Estimate]\nThis is roughly HALF the reported FCF ($337M TTM). Elevated SBC permanently\ndilutes shareholders \u2014 net share issuance +4.4% in FY25 [Actual].\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating leases: FY25 operating leases $41M [Actual].\nCapitalise as debt: +$41M [Assumption, 8x rent proxy excluded \u2014 small].\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data: Brand value implicit in 60%+ gross margin and 90%+\nwin-rate retention; no reliable separate value [Unconfirmed].\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC:\nAdjusted EBIT (adding back SBC) \u2248 $203M; adjusted NOPAT \u2248 $173M\nInvested capital \u2248 Equity $1,457M + Debt $716M \u2212 Cash+inv $1,484M = $689M\nAdjusted ROIC \u2248 25%    (GAAP ROIC \u2248 5%)\nGap > 5pts: SBC ($162M) is the dominant cause; adjusted ROIC overstates\nbecause SBC is a real ongoing cost paid in equity, not a capitalised asset.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (FY25):\nNI $70M + D&A $24M \u2212 maintenance capex ~$20M [Assumption] \u2212 \u0394WC (deferred\nrevenue effect) ~$0 = ~$74M; or OCF-based: OCF $301M \u2212 capex $6M \u2212 SBC $162M\n= $133M. I use the OCF-based owner earnings as the more reliable measure.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION ENGINE 1 (QUALITY COMPOUNDER DCF)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nLayer 0 selection: ARCHETYPE B \u2014 Quality Compounder (stable recurring rev,\nhigh switching costs, inflecting margins). NOT Option-Led (core revenue now\n~80% recurring, GM >60%, FCF+ 8 consecutive quarters [Actual]). Alternative\nengines would misprice: Engine 4 would overpay for AI optionality; Engine 2\nwould treat a low-cyclicality software business as a commodity cyclical.\n\nDiscount-rate derivation:\n\n| Input | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free (10Y UST) | 4.70% | [Actual] 2026-08-21 |\n| Equity Risk Premium | 4.5% | [Assumption] Damodaran-style |\n| Beta | 0.925 | [Estimate] vendor, window unstated |\n| Cost of Equity | 4.70 + 0.925\u00d74.5 = 8.86% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Pre-tax cost of debt | 5.5% | [Assumption] |\n| After-tax cost of debt | 4.3% | [Assumption] 21% tax |\n| Debt weight (D/D+E) | 4.3% | [Arithmetic] |\n| WACC | 0.957\u00d78.86 + 0.043\u00d74.30 = 8.7% | [Arithmetic] |\n\nNet cash: cash+ST inv+LT inv $1,146.8M (Apr-30-26, [Actual]) \u2212 vendor total\ndebt $716M [Estimate] \u2212 leases $41M [Actual] = $390M [Estimate]. The vendor\ndebt figure is the best available; the pack's own long-term series is\nincomplete (FY25 LT debt \"not available\") [Unconfirmed total-debt composition].\n\nFORECAST (Base case):\n\n| FY | Revenue ($M) | Growth | GAAP EBIT Margin | EBIT ($M) | Tax | NOPAT | D&A | Capex | FCF |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FY26E | 1,465 | +21.9% | 8.8% | 129 | 15% | 110 | 26 | 35 | 101 |\n| FY27E | 1,689 | +15.3% | 10.0% | 169 | 15% | 144 | 28 | 38 | 134 |\n| FY28E | 1,909 | +13.0% | 12.0% | 229 | 15% | 195 | 30 | 42 | 183 |\n| FY29E | 2,119 | +11.0% | 14.5% | 307 | 20% | 246 | 32 | 46 | 232 |\n| FY30E | 2,310 | +9.0% | 17.0% | 393 | 20% | 314 | 34 | 50 | 298 |\n| FY31E | 2,471 | +7.0% | 22.0% | 544 | 20% | 435 | 36 | 54 | 417 |\n\nForecast-driver sourcing:\n- FY26 revenue/EBIT/OCF: midpoint of company guidance [Actual].\n- FY27 growth 15.3%: consensus forward revenue growth in the data pack,\n  [Estimate \u2014 yfinance, horizon unstated]. FY27 is BEYOND hard guidance:\n  Q4 FY26 earnings occur 2026-09-03 (after T0); FY27 guidance NOT yet public\n  [Unconfirmed]. This is the central uncertainty of the whole analysis.\n- FY28\u2013FY31 growth: [Assumption] \u2014 mean-reverts toward the 9.7% insurtech\n  market CAGR [Estimate, Technavio]. Base case assumes successful share gain\n  but inevitable deceleration.\n- EBIT margins to 22% by FY31: [Assumption]. Requires non-GAAP margins to\n  reach ~33-35% AND SBC to fall from 13% of revenue toward ~8%. This is\n  already an aggressive assumption (current GAAP OI margin: 8.8%).\n- Tax 15% early (NOL-heavy), 20% late: [Assumption].\n- Capex includes capitalised software (repairing the pack's known P/P&E-only\n  understatement \u2014 vendor $20M vs filed $6M FY25): [Assumption].\n\nTerminal value (Base): FCF FY32 = $417M \u00d7 1.035 = $432M; TV = $432 /\n(0.087 \u2212 0.035) = $8.30B.\n\nDiscounted value: PV of FY26\u201331 FCFs = $963M; PV of TV = $5,030M.\nEnterprise value = $5,993M. Add net cash $390M = Equity $6,383M.\nPer share (85.5M diluted shares): $74.7 \u2192 BASE IV \u2248 $75.\n\nSENSITIVITY \u2014 beyond the guided year, the valuation is wildly sensitive to\nFY27 growth and terminal margin (this is the honest range):\n\n| Scenario / WACC | Terminal GAAP Margin | FY27 Growth | IV per share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bear | 15% | 12% | ~$39 |\n| Base | 22% | 15% | ~$75 |\n| Bull | 28% | 15% | ~$112 |\n| Optimistic extreme | 30% | 18% | ~$168 |\n| Market-implied at $189 | 30%+ | 20%+ | $189 |\n\nEven under my MOST optimistic reasonable case (WACC 8.0%, 30% terminal GAAP\nmargin, 18% FY27 growth), intrinsic value = ~$168 \u2014 still BELOW the market\nprice. The market is pricing a top-1-percentile outcome as certain.\n\nMultiples cross-check:\n- EV/Sales FY26E: $15.36B / $1.465B = 10.5\u00d7\n- Forward P/E (non-GAAP FY26E EPS ~$3.27): ~58\u00d7\n- EV/EBITDA FY26E: 99\u00d7\n- P/Owner-FCF TTM: ~$15.75B / ~$0.175B = 90\u00d7\nAll rich versus 10-yr history and quality-software peers.\n\nReverse DCF (what the market implies): At $189, EV = $15.36B. After PV of\nexplicit forecast FCFs (~$0.96B), required PV of TV = $14.4B \u2192 TV =\n$23.75B \u2192 terminal FCF FY32 = $1.24B. At a 25% true FCF margin (after SBC\nand capex \u2014 best-in-class), FY31 revenue must be \u2248 $4.8B \u2014 i.e., 21.7% CAGR\nfor five straight years. VERDICT: EXCESSIVE/UNREALISTIC as base case.\n\nTornado (Top-3 variables): 1) FY27 ARR/revenue growth, 2) Terminal GAAP\nmargin (i.e., SBC trajectory + non-GAAP margin), 3) WACC. These become the\nThesis KPIs.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n3.5-1. Competitive response: If Guidewire continues winning Tier-1 cloud\ndeals, Duck Creek, Majesco, and Sapiens will compete on price and speed;\nAccenture/Wipro SI leverage could shift margin. Impact: \u22121 (moderate, slow).\n3.5-2. Reflexivity: High stock price = cheap acquisition currency + SBC\nattracts talent (positive). A falling stock would tighten the SBC flywheel\n(negative). Net: neutral-to-positive while the stock is high; fragile if\nthe multiple resets.\n3.5-3. Customer behavior: Insurers move from early-adopter cloud pilots\n(2021-24) to core-system mainstream replacement (2025-28); the largest\ncarriers have multi-year rollouts. LTV/CAC is excellent (20+ yr contracts);\nchurn is historically near-zero but unverifiable from the pack [Unconfirmed].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nPrior = base rates (Layer 1.5): 40-50% sustained success.\nUpdate: Q3 FY26 ARR +19%, FY26 guide raised (revenue, OI, OCF) [Actual] \u2014\npositive update; 10Y yield 4.7% (hawkish) \u2014 negative update for long-duration\nmultiples; stock \u221213% post-earnings on ARR guide [Estimate, news] \u2014 market\nalready updating growth down.\n\nPosterior scenario set (probabilities sum to 100%):\n\n| Scenario | Probability | IV | 2-yr Price Target | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bear: growth 12\u21924%, margin 15%, WACC 9.3% | 30% | ~$39 | $55 | ARR decelerates <15%, SBC stays 12%, macro recession |\n| Base: growth 15\u21927%, margin 22%, WACC 8.7% | 45% | ~$75 | $95 | Smooth cloud conversion; SBC grinds down |\n| Bull: growth 15\u21929%, margin 28%, WACC 8.0% | 25% | ~$112 | $150 | GAAP margins converge to non-GAAP; buyback compounds |\n\nExpected IV = 0.30\u00d739 + 0.45\u00d775 + 0.25\u00d7112 = 11.7 + 33.8 + 28.0 = $73.5\n80% CI: $39 \u2013 $112. Single-point IV: $73.5.\n\nMargin of Safety = ($73.5 \u2212 $189.19) / $73.5 = \u2212157% \u2192 NONE.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nMarket-implied horizon at $189: the price implies >20 years of flawless\ncompounding at WACC 8.7% (or equivalently a $4.8B revenue, 25%-margin\nterminal business). My horizon: 5-6 years. Spread = \u221214 years \u2014 the market\nis NOT leaving a horizon arbitrage; it is pre-paying for a full decade of\noutperformance. No arbitrage exists; the risk is asymmetric against the\nbuyer at T0.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u255